
  [34mconst[39m loginRules = { [36musername[39m: [[33mrequired[39m([31m"Username is required"[39m)],
                       [36mpassword[39m: [[33mrequired[39m([31m"Password is required"[39m)] };
  The submit handler calls [38;5;153mvalidate(loginRules, { username, password })[39m and, on failure, logs the per-field errors —
  same [38;5;153mconsole.error[39m behavior as today, just per field. No visible behavior change for a valid login.

  [1m[38;5;153mindex.html[0m changes one line: [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m → [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m. Consequence worth knowing:
  module scripts are blocked by CORS on [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so opening the page by double-clicking stops working. You'd need
  [38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m or similar. That's the one real cost of the ES-module choice.

  [1mWhat I'm not building:[0m the signup and profile-edit forms. You named them as motivation, not as scope, so the
  deliverable is the module plus login migrated onto it. If you want one of them built as a proof that the abstraction
  actually holds up, say so and I'll fold it in — it's a reasonable thing to want, and a second real consumer is the
  only honest test of a reuse claim.

  [1mScope note:[0m [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m are untouched. They're unrelated Node code.

  [1mTooling[0m

  The repo has no test runner, linter, formatter, or scripts block. A pure, DOM-free validator is about the most
  testable thing you can write, and it's cheapest to set this up now rather than after three forms depend on it. Node's
  built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m runs ES modules with zero dependencies, which fits a repo that currently has none.

